                <pb facs="0486" n="342" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg342"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/434: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-11-04T16:00:00+08:00">November 4, 1929—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 5—10:20 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>961. My 951, November 2, 3 p.m. Following from American Consul at Harbin: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“November 4, 3 p.m. Fuchin some days ago occupied by Soviet forces but
                            now again in the hands of Chinese troops. This raid and loss of
                            practically entire Chinese gunboat fleet have depressed all local
                            Chinese circles and produced panicky feeling at Harbin, inhabitants
                            Taheiho evacuated, they and other residents along the Amur River fear
                            Soviet offensive when Amur freezes which will be within a period of a
                            week.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
